EXT. DRIFTING ICE FLOE - DAY

A slab of white, a hundred feet across, adrift in a grey sea. Fog presses on every side. The ice groans.

LAWRENCE DEVEREUX, 50s, polar surveyor, in a heavy wool coat caked with frost, kneels by a crate of instruments. His beard is white with rime.

ABEL TSEGAYE, 30s, his assistant, stands at the edge of the floe, peering into the fog. A rope wound about his chest.

LAWRENCE
Abel, stay back from the edge.

ABEL
There is water on the east side.

LAWRENCE
There is water on every side.

ABEL
The ice is thinner there. I heard it crack.

LAWRENCE
Then we stay on the west.

He rises, stiff-limbed. He picks up a compass. The needle spins, useless.

ABEL
How long has the floe been drifting?

LAWRENCE
Since Tuesday.

ABEL
We should signal.

LAWRENCE
With what? A flare?

ABEL
There are two flares.

LAWRENCE
There is one. The other went down with the sled.

Abel turns. Fog swallows the sea.

ABEL
You didn't tell me.

LAWRENCE
You didn't ask.

A deep CRACK runs through the ice. Both men freeze. A fissure opens ten feet from Lawrence's boots, black water welling.

ABEL
Lawrence!

He reaches him. Hauls him back. The fissure widens to a yard.

LAWRENCE
Cut the rope.

ABEL
No.

LAWRENCE
Abel, if it takes me, it takes you.

ABEL
Then it takes us both. Pull!

Lawrence scrambles up. They collapse on the solid ice. The gap stops, a few feet wide. Water slaps the edges.

LAWRENCE
(breathless)
One flare.

ABEL
One flare.

Lawrence digs in his pack. Pulls out a dented red tube. His hand shakes.

LAWRENCE
You do it.

ABEL
Why?

LAWRENCE
Because he who lights it will be the one they find first.

Abel takes the flare. Pulls the cap. A plume of crimson smoke bursts into the fog, bright as blood on snow.
